# `actuarial::mortality::from_lx`

Builds a mortality law from the lives of a life table.

## Signature

```cpp
actuarial::Mortality actuarial::mortality::from_lx(const pluss::Series &lx);
```

## What It Does

Takes {math}`l_0,l_1,\ldots,l_n`, the expected number of lives at each age, and stores {math}`q_{x+k}=1-l_{k+1}/l_k`. The horizon is one less than the number of values, since `lx` includes the starting cohort. The scale of the table does not matter, only the ratios. Call it for a published life table; [`Mortality::life_table`](Mortality/life_table.md) is the inverse. A table that ends at zero is accepted and gives a final probability of one.

## Parameters

`lx`
: One-dimensional series of lives, positive at the first age and non-increasing.

## Returns

A defined `Mortality` with horizon `lx.numel() − 1`; a single value gives horizon 0.

## Errors

Throws `qx_lx: lx[0] must be > 0` for a table that starts at zero and `qx_lx: lx must be non-increasing` for one that rises.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a published table

Lives at five ages and the probabilities they imply.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality m = actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({100000.0, 99000.0, 97812.0, 96347.0, 94516.0}));
  out.writeln("horizon =", m.horizon());
  out.writeln("qx      =", m.qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 2: the scale does not matter

A table of 1,000 lives and one of 100,000 give the same law.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({1000.0, 990.0, 978.12})).qx().items<double>());
  out.writeln(actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({100000.0, 99000.0, 97812.0})).qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: a table that dies out

Ending at zero gives a final probability of one.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0, 50.0, 0.0})).qx().items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: a single value

One age and no years is a law with horizon 0.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  out.writeln(actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0})).horizon());
}
```

### Example 5: the errors

A table that rises or starts at zero is refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({100.0, 90.0, 95.0})); });
  report([&] { (void)actuarial::mortality::from_lx(pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.0})); });
}
```

